WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.960
To this point, in learning the different&nbsp;
ways in which we can use criteria in queries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.400
we have just used a single criterion. Many&nbsp;
queries require multiple criteria and the key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:16.400
to using multiple criteria is to use the correct&nbsp;
keywords given a situation. Here is an example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.320
I have a query with people and locations, and I&nbsp;
am looking for anyone with the last name of Adams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.120
in the city of Los Angeles. I already&nbsp;
have a WHERE clause started to restrict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.840
the query to return people with the last&nbsp;
name of Adams. I will execute the query,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:39.200
and so far, everything works. When using&nbsp;
multiple criteria, the two main keywords&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:49.120
to use between criterion pieces are AND and OR. We&nbsp;
just have to ask ourselves this: Are we trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:55.920
narrow our results further? If the answer is&nbsp;
yes, then AND is the keyword we want to use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:02.720
We do want to narrow this list to show only&nbsp;
those with the last name of Adams in Los Angeles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:12.960
To the existing WHERE clause, I will add AND&nbsp;
City = 'Los Angeles'. I will execute this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:20.880
and we have just one record&nbsp;
that fits the criteria.

